WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:02.090 --> 00:00:05.480
Human beings have adapted the world around
us to suit our needs.

00:00:05.480 --> 00:00:10.460
It is our intelligence that allowed us to
learn tool building, agriculture and language

00:00:10.460 --> 00:00:14.980
and so become far advanced than any of our
fellow creatures.

00:00:14.980 --> 00:00:21.060
But, that’s not to say that some animals
don’t have wonderful abilities that we can

00:00:21.060 --> 00:00:25.500
only begin to marvel at.

00:00:25.500 --> 00:00:33.090
Thanks to the work of medical science and
nutrition, we are living longer and longer,

00:00:33.090 --> 00:00:38.100
with life expectancy increasing by 1 year,
every 5 years.

00:00:38.100 --> 00:00:43.350
We are able to survive some pretty extreme
places on earth but we have nothing on a little

00:00:43.350 --> 00:00:45.680
guy called a Tardigrade.

00:00:45.680 --> 00:00:51.060
Also known as Water Bears, or the super cute
name “Moss Piglets”, these segmented micro

00:00:51.060 --> 00:00:58.350
animals are typically 0.5 millimetres in length
and can be found in virtually every environment

00:00:58.350 --> 00:00:59.539
in the world.

00:00:59.539 --> 00:01:04.360
Their ability to survive is, to be honest,
utterly ridiculous.

00:01:04.360 --> 00:01:09.520
They are the supermen of the animal kingdom,
if Superman was a tiny 8 legged bear made

00:01:09.520 --> 00:01:10.520
of pillows.

00:01:10.520 --> 00:01:12.539
Think Canada is cold?

00:01:12.539 --> 00:01:21.920
Well these bad boys can manage at -272°C,
that’s only 1 degree above absolute zero,

00:01:21.920 --> 00:01:27.310
where everything, right down to the nuclear
level, stops moving.

00:01:27.310 --> 00:01:31.090
Of course, they don’t just put on a hat
and get on with their shopping, they are in

00:01:31.090 --> 00:01:36.950
a state of cryptobiosis – like the deep
hibernation you see in sci-fi films.

00:01:36.950 --> 00:01:39.899
And they can only last at these levels for
a few minutes.

00:01:39.899 --> 00:01:45.399
But bring the heat back up and they’ll be
out and about again, getting up to mischief.

00:01:45.399 --> 00:01:51.219
If they fancy a bit of sun after a winter
snooze, then you can crank it up to 150°C

00:01:51.219 --> 00:01:56.869
and they’ll still be knocking back the margaritas.

00:01:56.869 --> 00:02:02.499
They can take pressure 6 times what you’d
find at the bottom of our deepest ocean and

00:02:02.499 --> 00:02:08.239
can handle doses of ionising radiation so
high, that just a fraction of a percent of

00:02:08.239 --> 00:02:13.239
it would turn us into dribbling mutants while
our cells collapsed.

00:02:13.239 --> 00:02:20.150
They can even survive in space for almost
2 weeks and can survive without food or water,

00:02:20.150 --> 00:02:22.290
for over 30 years.

00:02:22.290 --> 00:02:24.650
They would probably prefer not to though.

00:02:24.650 --> 00:02:30.670
But if you want real immortality, then scientists
are now looking at the Scarlet Jellyfish.

00:02:30.670 --> 00:02:36.540
Its gelatinous body may not offer much protection
but it has an incredible trick.

00:02:36.540 --> 00:02:42.010
If it’s injured, it sinks to the sea floor
and returns to its juvenile state, called

00:02:42.010 --> 00:02:43.239
a polyp.

00:02:43.239 --> 00:02:49.940
From here, it regrows back to an adult jellyfish,
with the slate wiped clean, effectively granting

00:02:49.940 --> 00:02:52.290
it biological immortality.

00:02:52.290 --> 00:02:54.590
The whole process takes about 2 months.

00:02:54.590 --> 00:02:59.040
So maybe one day, if you break a leg or two,
you just have to pop back to kindergarten

00:02:59.040 --> 00:03:03.580
for a few semesters whilst you regenerate,
it might help improve your terrible handwriting

00:03:03.580 --> 00:03:04.840
as well.

00:03:04.840 --> 00:03:08.349
When we think about light, we think about
what we can see.

00:03:08.349 --> 00:03:13.349
But the visible spectrum is actually a very
small part of the wider range.

00:03:13.349 --> 00:03:18.970
And even within the visible spectrum, our
ability to separate colours is actually quite

00:03:18.970 --> 00:03:20.299
limited.

00:03:20.299 --> 00:03:25.629
In the back of your eye, the retina, you process
the light through cells called photoreceptors.

00:03:25.629 --> 00:03:29.730
There are two main types; rods and cones.

00:03:29.730 --> 00:03:36.061
The rods process just black and white so they
work well in low-light situations and they

00:03:36.061 --> 00:03:38.269
make up most of your peripheral vision.

00:03:38.269 --> 00:03:42.220
You’ll notice that you don’t see much
colour around the edges of your vision.

00:03:42.220 --> 00:03:46.359
You’re doing it now, aren’t you; trying
to look out the side of your eyes?

00:03:46.359 --> 00:03:51.200
In the middle of the retina, where most of
the light comes in, you have a higher concentration

00:03:51.200 --> 00:03:53.090
of cone cells.

00:03:53.090 --> 00:04:00.209
Cone cells process colour and there are three
types; one for long, medium and short wavelengths.

00:04:00.209 --> 00:04:04.749
This is all a colour is, a different wave
length.

00:04:04.749 --> 00:04:10.680
Long are reds, medium are yellow and green
and short are blue.

00:04:10.680 --> 00:04:15.280
The Mantis Shrimp has 12 different cones.

00:04:15.280 --> 00:04:21.479
If we had eyes like this, we would be able
to see huge differences between every shade.

00:04:21.479 --> 00:04:25.190
It would make arguments over what colour to
paint the bedroom last for a lifetime.

00:04:25.190 --> 00:04:30.849
It’s bad enough that there’s paint called
elephant breath, just imagine if there were

00:04:30.849 --> 00:04:33.090
46 different shades of it.

00:04:33.090 --> 00:04:38.960
But, oddly, shrimps’ colour perception is
not much better than our own since they don’t

00:04:38.960 --> 00:04:42.139
have the brain power to decipher all that
information.

00:04:42.139 --> 00:04:46.950
Their amazing eyes just do most of the work
so their little brains don’t have to.

00:04:46.950 --> 00:04:50.210
That is not the only quirk of their evolution
though.

00:04:50.210 --> 00:04:55.700
Like their cousin, the Pistol Shrimp, they
can punch so rapidly with their claws that

00:04:55.700 --> 00:04:59.069
they have been known to smash aquarium glass.

00:04:59.069 --> 00:05:06.319
Finally, on vision, the Pit Viper may have
slightly worse colour detection than we do

00:05:06.319 --> 00:05:12.599
but, they have evolved a new sensor pit, between
the eyes and the nostrils, that allows them

00:05:12.599 --> 00:05:15.160
to see infra-red light.

00:05:15.160 --> 00:05:21.539
This means they can spot prey and be warned
of predators, no matter how dark it gets.

00:05:21.539 --> 00:05:26.500
The benefits of technology mean that we can
enhance or replace many of our body parts.

00:05:26.500 --> 00:05:32.949
But these are man-made, these kind of mechanics
don’t occur naturally, do they?

00:05:32.949 --> 00:05:34.750
This is the Issus Leafhopper.

00:05:34.750 --> 00:05:41.590
It’s a small, plant-sucking bug and it has
evolved functioning mechanical gears.

00:05:41.590 --> 00:05:44.710
This allows it to jump extremely rapidly.

00:05:44.710 --> 00:05:52.789
It may only move at 5 m/s but it accelerates
in under a millisecond, at a force of over

00:05:52.789 --> 00:05:54.009
500 g’s.

00:05:54.009 --> 00:05:58.470
To give you some context, one g is the normal
gravity you feel on earth.

00:05:58.470 --> 00:06:01.930
An astronaut leaving earth experiences about
3 g.

00:06:01.930 --> 00:06:05.470
So what is the point of these biological cogs?

00:06:05.470 --> 00:06:07.970
What purpose do they achieve?

00:06:07.970 --> 00:06:12.910
Well the power comes from both legs and at
such an incredible acceleration, that if there

00:06:12.910 --> 00:06:17.710
was a tiny difference between them, it would
be impossible to control the direction.

00:06:17.710 --> 00:06:23.379
Imagine if astronauts were in a rocket with
two engines and each were firing at different

00:06:23.379 --> 00:06:26.710
power levels; Houston, we have a problem.

00:06:26.710 --> 00:06:32.160
So, to ensure that the Leafhoppers aren’t
constantly crashing into walls, head-butting

00:06:32.160 --> 00:06:37.680
people in bars, or accidentally committing
suicide, the gears act to perfectly synchronise

00:06:37.680 --> 00:06:39.300
their leaps.

00:06:39.300 --> 00:06:44.710
The cogwheel turtle also has an obvious gear
shape that has grown naturally, but it has

00:06:44.710 --> 00:06:46.470
no mechanical purpose.

00:06:46.470 --> 00:06:49.540
It just looks like he’s listened to too
much Marilyn Manson.

00:06:49.540 --> 00:06:51.090
Look at this guy (show image of guy in camo-trousers).

00:06:51.090 --> 00:06:52.540
I know, where are his legs?

00:06:52.540 --> 00:06:54.720
They’re completely invisible.

00:06:54.720 --> 00:07:01.360
Now, our level of camouflage is pretty lame,
but there are creatures out there that can

00:07:01.360 --> 00:07:06.160
change shape and colour in an almost magical
way.

00:07:06.160 --> 00:07:12.050
Firstly, there is the chameleon of course,
everyone’s favourite hide and seek contestant.

00:07:12.050 --> 00:07:17.259
They can change colour and pattern in order
to send messages, such as aggression or submission,

00:07:17.259 --> 00:07:19.460
as well as camouflage.

00:07:19.460 --> 00:07:23.470
They sometimes use it as a way of controlling
the heat they absorb too.

00:07:23.470 --> 00:07:24.819
How does it work?

00:07:24.819 --> 00:07:29.139
We used to think they just had amazing control
of the colour pigments in their skin.

00:07:29.139 --> 00:07:32.650
But it turns out to be far smarter than that.

00:07:32.650 --> 00:07:37.080
In the past couple of years we have discovered
that one of the layers of their skin is made

00:07:37.080 --> 00:07:40.420
up of a lattice of crystals called guanine.

00:07:40.420 --> 00:07:45.180
We all contain guanine as it is a nitrogen
based compound that is one of the bases of

00:07:45.180 --> 00:07:49.659
DNA and RNA, so, pretty important stuff.

00:07:49.659 --> 00:07:54.229
Chameleons can stretch this lattice layer
so it reflects light differently.

00:07:54.229 --> 00:07:59.620
When the lattice is excited, it stretches
and this reflects longer wavelengths, such

00:07:59.620 --> 00:08:02.030
as yellows, oranges and reds.

00:08:02.030 --> 00:08:07.500
In a relaxed state, it reflects the shorter
wavelengths, the blues and greens.

00:08:07.500 --> 00:08:12.310
Their skin also has a yellow pigment which
is why their base colour is often green, since

00:08:12.310 --> 00:08:18.530
the relaxed lattice gives off blues and this
mixes with the yellow pigment to create green.

00:08:18.530 --> 00:08:23.210
A far prettier colour changing animal is the
Golden Tortoise Beetle.

00:08:23.210 --> 00:08:28.219
They look like jewellery most of the time
but not only is their metallic gold colouring

00:08:28.219 --> 00:08:34.480
pretty spectacular, they are very rare in
the insect world as they can change colour.

00:08:34.480 --> 00:08:39.080
The layers of their shells contain nano-grooves,
that are normally filled with a light red

00:08:39.080 --> 00:08:41.940
liquid that gives them their golden shine.

00:08:41.940 --> 00:08:47.850
When this liquid is drained, the lower, darker
red layers become visible so, when you watch

00:08:47.850 --> 00:08:50.790
them, they turn from gold to red.

00:08:50.790 --> 00:08:55.870
This happens when they are agitated, and often
during sex, just like us humans, but it’s

00:08:55.870 --> 00:08:58.040
believed to have another benefit.

00:08:58.040 --> 00:09:04.170
Red shelled bugs, like ladybirds, or ladybugs
in the US, aren’t popular food for birds.

00:09:04.170 --> 00:09:09.910
So maybe the Golden Tortoise Beetles are mimicking
this to avoid being lunch.

00:09:09.910 --> 00:09:14.350
Even their gold colour could make them hard
to spot, as the bright reflection would throw

00:09:14.350 --> 00:09:16.590
off their avian enemies.

00:09:16.590 --> 00:09:22.861
Now colour is one thing, but the mimic octopus
can transform its whole body to look like

00:09:22.861 --> 00:09:25.850
a wide range of other local creatures.

00:09:25.850 --> 00:09:31.100
It impersonates sea snakes and jellyfish,
amongst other things, so that its predators

00:09:31.100 --> 00:09:32.100
avoid it.

00:09:32.100 --> 00:09:38.940
And it also uses impersonation to catch its
own food, sometimes walking like a crab so

00:09:38.940 --> 00:09:45.290
it can attract a mate and then pouncing before
the crab realises it’s too late.

00:09:45.290 --> 00:09:48.970
Aquatic tinder is a scary place – “Free
for a drink tomorrow?

00:09:48.970 --> 00:09:52.600
Okay, but promise me you’re not secretly
an octopus?”

00:09:52.600 --> 00:09:57.670
Opossums find the world in general a scary
place, which is why they are famous for thanatosis,

00:09:57.670 --> 00:10:00.990
or “playing dead” to you and me.

00:10:00.990 --> 00:10:06.770
But when real danger strikes, they have a
nifty trick up their scruffy little sleeves.

00:10:06.770 --> 00:10:12.050
Opossums can immunise themselves against toxins
such as snake venom and plant stings.

00:10:12.050 --> 00:10:18.030
They produce a protein called the Lethal Toxin
Neutralizing Factor, LTNF for short, that

00:10:18.030 --> 00:10:22.840
does exactly what its name suggests, neutralises
toxins.

00:10:22.840 --> 00:10:27.750
For snake bites, this means they can counteract
both the part that stops your heart and the

00:10:27.750 --> 00:10:30.620
haemorrhaging part, which makes you keep on
bleeding.

00:10:30.620 --> 00:10:34.760
For humans, the current way of dealing with
a snake bite, is through anti-venoms.

00:10:34.760 --> 00:10:39.450
You have to take a sample of each specific
snake’s venom and then inject a small dose

00:10:39.450 --> 00:10:42.410
into another animal, such as a goat or a horse.

00:10:42.410 --> 00:10:47.230
Then, we can extract a serum and use it to
fight the poison when a human is bitten.

00:10:47.230 --> 00:10:51.810
This procedure is far from perfect as you
need to keep a range of different anti-venoms

00:10:51.810 --> 00:10:54.460
and often these are only at major hospitals.

00:10:54.460 --> 00:10:59.550
And, I may be wrong, but you probably won’t
get attacked by a snake in a hospital, they’re

00:10:59.550 --> 00:11:02.020
more of an outdoorsy type.

00:11:02.020 --> 00:11:08.250
So naturally research into this opossum protein
could be very valuable as it’s already been

00:11:08.250 --> 00:11:13.390
shown that mice can be injected with it and
develop similar self-immunisation skills to

00:11:13.390 --> 00:11:14.660
the opossum.

00:11:14.660 --> 00:11:17.340
A human ready version may not be so far away.

00:11:17.340 --> 00:11:21.640
Let’s just hope it doesn’t make us all
play dead too, the streets would be littered

00:11:21.640 --> 00:11:26.330
with people falling over every time a car
horn sounds.

00:11:26.330 --> 00:11:31.050
Self-immunisation is one thing but our next
creature takes it to a whole new level.

00:11:31.050 --> 00:11:36.590
This baby-faced little salamander is called
the Axolotl and it has an astonishing ability

00:11:36.590 --> 00:11:42.320
to regenerate whole body parts again and again.

00:11:42.320 --> 00:11:47.640
Many other salamander types have some regeneration
ability, such as their tails, but these guys

00:11:47.640 --> 00:11:52.130
can grow back whole legs, spines, and even
parts of their brain.

00:11:52.130 --> 00:11:56.850
And there’s not even any scarring, it’s
just like reloading from a save point in a

00:11:56.850 --> 00:11:58.020
video game.

00:11:58.020 --> 00:11:59.770
Lost your leg in a bike accident?

00:11:59.770 --> 00:12:01.850
No problem, just reboot.

00:12:01.850 --> 00:12:07.890
They’re also over 1000 times more resistant
to cancer than mammals are, so they’re a

00:12:07.890 --> 00:12:09.190
pretty healthy bunch.

00:12:09.190 --> 00:12:11.690
They also never smoke and only drink in moderation.

00:12:11.690 --> 00:12:15.330
There is actually a reason for their babyish
face.

00:12:15.330 --> 00:12:20.340
They are one of a few unique species since
they exhibit neoteny.

00:12:20.340 --> 00:12:25.280
This means that they don’t go through a
change to reach adulthood, or sexual maturity

00:12:25.280 --> 00:12:27.060
to be more precise.

00:12:27.060 --> 00:12:32.300
Most animals have a metamorphosis that changes
their shape, colouring and physical characteristics.

00:12:32.300 --> 00:12:37.350
A human adult isn’t just an inflated baby,
the proportions are all different.

00:12:37.350 --> 00:12:43.580
The head shrinks in comparison to the body,
limbs lengthen, body hair grows and so on.

00:12:43.580 --> 00:12:49.160
The same is true for most species; just look
at tadpoles becoming frogs, larvae becoming

00:12:49.160 --> 00:12:52.810
flies and seahorses growing into full sized
horses.

00:12:52.810 --> 00:12:55.830
I’m not sure about that last one actually.

00:12:55.830 --> 00:12:59.570
But the Axolotl stays the same, it just gets
larger.

00:12:59.570 --> 00:13:04.690
Most salamanders leave the water and grow
fully developed lungs but the Axolotl stays

00:13:04.690 --> 00:13:09.210
in the water and that pretty frill around
its neck is its gills.

00:13:09.210 --> 00:13:13.300
They do have rudimentary lungs but they don’t
really use them.

00:13:13.300 --> 00:13:18.040
Scientists have found that you can induce
a change into adulthood in axolotls, where

00:13:18.040 --> 00:13:20.420
they become terrestrial salamanders.

00:13:20.420 --> 00:13:24.380
It’s done by injecting iodine into their
thyroid gland.

00:13:24.380 --> 00:13:29.031
Whereas, us humans are forced to grow up by
being sent to our rooms and not allowed to

00:13:29.031 --> 00:13:30.700
have any ice cream.

00:13:30.700 --> 00:13:34.680
What ability do you think ties all of these
animals together?

00:13:34.680 --> 00:13:35.680
Any guesses?

00:13:35.680 --> 00:13:37.660
Are they super-fast?

00:13:37.660 --> 00:13:38.660
Super-smart?

00:13:38.660 --> 00:13:39.930
Do they look good in denim?

00:13:39.930 --> 00:13:41.610
Are they great at doing Sudoku?

00:13:41.610 --> 00:13:42.970
Give up?

00:13:42.970 --> 00:13:46.490
Their special skill is electroreception.

00:13:46.490 --> 00:13:51.720
They have the ability to detect electrical
fields and to use them as a sense, much like

00:13:51.720 --> 00:13:53.400
we use hearing and vision.

00:13:53.400 --> 00:13:59.360
It’s almost exclusive to aquatic animals
since water, especially salt water, is a far

00:13:59.360 --> 00:14:01.400
better conductor than air.

00:14:01.400 --> 00:14:07.070
But bees use electrical fields and charge
in a very unique way.

00:14:07.070 --> 00:14:11.980
Plants are usually negatively charged and
bees can become very positively charged.

00:14:11.980 --> 00:14:17.570
We’ve seen them produce voltages of 450
volts during tests.

00:14:17.570 --> 00:14:22.000
This happens because they collide with dust
particles and small molecules as they fly.

00:14:22.000 --> 00:14:27.440
The collisions knock away electrons and leave
positively charged particles stuck to the

00:14:27.440 --> 00:14:28.440
bee.

00:14:28.440 --> 00:14:33.270
Their waxy cuticles are very well insulated,
meaning they can build up a surprising amount

00:14:33.270 --> 00:14:34.600
of charge.

00:14:34.600 --> 00:14:36.700
This creates certain benefits.

00:14:36.700 --> 00:14:41.330
Firstly, they become “stickier” since
they attract the negatively charged pollen

00:14:41.330 --> 00:14:42.330
particles.

00:14:42.330 --> 00:14:49.210
But, incredibly, they also develop a communication
with the plants and we believe they can read

00:14:49.210 --> 00:14:54.820
which flowers have recently been visited by
other bees, due to the change in their electric

00:14:54.820 --> 00:14:55.820
field.

00:14:55.820 --> 00:15:01.170
It’s basically floral advertising, so bees
can bumble around, looking in the shop windows

00:15:01.170 --> 00:15:05.540
to find what they want, without having to
go in to every store.

00:15:05.540 --> 00:15:08.440
And the electro bee magic doesn’t end there.

00:15:08.440 --> 00:15:13.660
Uwe Greggers, a neurobiologist from the Free
university of Berlin, has been studying the

00:15:13.660 --> 00:15:15.410
famous bee dance.

00:15:15.410 --> 00:15:20.920
We’ve known about their little waggle since
the 1920’s but Greggers now thinks that

00:15:20.920 --> 00:15:27.410
the bees are actually creating a change in
the electric field which other bees read through

00:15:27.410 --> 00:15:28.990
their antennae.

00:15:28.990 --> 00:15:35.150
Right now, all we know is that they CAN do
this, we don’t know for sure if they really

00:15:35.150 --> 00:15:36.150
are.

00:15:36.150 --> 00:15:38.450
Their dance still remains a mystery.

00:15:38.450 --> 00:15:44.070
The platypus is part of a group of egg laying
mammals, called monotremes, and they are the

00:15:44.070 --> 00:15:51.430
only creatures in the entire class known to
electrolocate, aside from one species of dolphin.

00:15:51.430 --> 00:15:56.520
These weird little duck faced otter monsters
close their eyes and ears when they dive and

00:15:56.520 --> 00:16:02.880
use this sense to detect the electric currents
produced in the muscles of its prey.

00:16:02.880 --> 00:16:06.290
This is why their heads sweep side to side,
like a sensor.

00:16:06.290 --> 00:16:08.920
They also have ankle spurs that produce venom.

00:16:08.920 --> 00:16:12.730
When you combine it with their egg laying,
it begins to seem like someone got very confused

00:16:12.730 --> 00:16:17.570
when they were putting the platypus together
and just grabbed whatever parts they could

00:16:17.570 --> 00:16:18.570
find.

00:16:18.570 --> 00:16:22.380
With all these amazing abilities, you might
be sitting there feeling jealous of the animal

00:16:22.380 --> 00:16:24.520
kingdom and all its wonders.

00:16:24.520 --> 00:16:28.710
Why can’t we jump like lightning, heal ourselves
and see in the dark?

00:16:28.710 --> 00:16:30.380
It’s just not fair.

00:16:30.380 --> 00:16:36.430
So, to make yourself feel better, go play
on your iPad, loudly, in front of your hamster

00:16:36.430 --> 00:16:40.620
and tell him that he can’t have a go because
he’s stupid.

